window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-bio-bio-classification","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Biological Classification · Practice","chapter":"Biological Classification","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which group of archaebacteria is responsible for producing methane (biogas) from the dung of cows and buffaloes?","image":"","options":["Halophiles","Methanogens","Thermoacidophiles","Cyanobacteria"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Methanogens live in the gut of ruminants such as cows and buffaloes and produce methane from their dung. Halophiles and thermoacidophiles are archaebacteria of salty and hot-spring habitats. Cyanobacteria are photosynthetic eubacteria.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Cuscuta is cited in NCERT as an example of a plant that is partially heterotrophic. How does it obtain part of its nutrition?","image":"","options":["As an insectivorous plant that traps insects","As a parasite on other plants","As a saprophyte absorbing dead organic matter","By forming mycorrhiza with fungi"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT names Cuscuta as a parasite. Bladderwort and Venus fly trap are its examples of insectivorous plants, so option 1 is the trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In ascomycetes and basidiomycetes, a cell may contain two separate haploid nuclei for some time during the sexual cycle.\nReason (R): In these fungi, karyogamy does not immediately follow plasmogamy, so an intervening n + n stage occurs.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In these fungi, plasmogamy is followed by a delay before karyogamy. This produces the dikaryon (n + n), in which each cell carries two unfused nuclei. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"An association between roots of higher plants and fungi is called","image":"","options":["Lichen","Fern","Mycorrhiza","BGA"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Mycorrhiza is a symbiotic association between fungi and the roots of higher plants. A lichen is an association of a fungus with an alga.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 2 (MCQ Q5)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): All protozoans are heterotrophs, living either as predators or as parasites.\nReason (R): Protozoans are believed to be primitive relatives of animals.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states both facts separately. Being heterotrophic is a feature of protozoans, and being considered primitive relatives of animals is an evolutionary interpretation that does not cause their mode of nutrition. The trap is assuming that an animal-like link explains heterotrophy.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Viruses that infect plants generally have single-stranded RNA, whereas bacteriophages are usually double-stranded DNA viruses.\nStatement II: Some animal viruses carry both RNA and DNA together as their genetic material.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Plant viruses generally have ssRNA and bacteriophages usually have dsDNA, so Statement I is correct. Statement II is wrong because no virus contains both RNA and DNA. Animal viruses have either ssRNA or dsRNA, or else dsDNA.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Deuteromycetes are commonly called 'imperfect fungi' because:","image":"","options":["Their mycelium is aseptate and coenocytic","Only their asexual or vegetative phases are known","They lack a cell wall made of chitin","They are always obligate parasites"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Deuteromycetes are called imperfect fungi because only their asexual or vegetative phases are known. Their mycelium is actually septate and branched, and many of them are decomposers.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"In slime moulds, the term 'plasmodium' refers to:","image":"","options":["The malarial parasite that infects slime moulds","An aggregation formed under suitable conditions that may spread over several feet","A resistant spore formed during unfavourable conditions","The fruiting body that bears spores at its tip"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Under suitable conditions, slime moulds form an aggregation called a plasmodium, which can grow and spread over several feet. Do not confuse it with Plasmodium, the sporozoan that causes malaria.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"The infectious agent discovered by T.O. Diener in 1971, which causes potato spindle tuber disease, is best described as:","image":"","options":["A virus with a single-stranded RNA genome enclosed in a capsid","An abnormally folded protein without nucleic acid","A free RNA of low molecular weight lacking a protein coat","A double-stranded DNA enclosed in a polyhedral capsid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Diener's viroid is free, low molecular weight RNA with no protein coat, which is why it is smaller than a virus. An abnormally folded protein describes a prion, not a viroid.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following organisms belong to Kingdom Protista in Whittaker's system?\nEuglena, Gonyaulax, Nostoc, Paramoecium, Plasmodium (malarial parasite), Trypanosoma, Anabaena, Yeast, Mycoplasma","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Euglena, Gonyaulax, Paramoecium, Plasmodium and Trypanosoma are unicellular eukaryotes, so they are protists (5 in all). Nostoc, Anabaena and Mycoplasma are prokaryotes (Monera), and yeast is a unicellular fungus.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about dinoflagellates are correct?\n(i) They are mostly marine.\n(ii) Their cell wall has stiff cellulose plates on the outer surface.\n(iii) Both of their flagella lie in the transverse furrow between the wall plates.\n(iv) They may appear yellow, green, brown, blue or red depending on their main pigments.\n(v) Their walls form two overlapping silica shells that fit together like a soap box.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii) and (iv) are correct. Statement (iii) is wrong because one flagellum lies longitudinally and only the other lies transversely. Statement (v) describes diatoms, not dinoflagellates.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about Aristotle's attempt at classification is correct?","image":"","options":["He classified plants into trees, shrubs and herbs using simple morphological characters.","He classified plants into algae, bryophytes and pteridophytes based on their life cycles.","He divided animals into vertebrates and invertebrates based on the presence of a backbone.","He grouped all organisms into prokaryotes and eukaryotes based on cell structure."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Aristotle used simple morphological characters, so he grouped plants into trees, shrubs and herbs. He divided animals by whether they had red blood, not by the presence of a backbone. Cell-structure criteria came much later.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Rhizopus\n(B) Albugo\n(C) Penicillium\n(D) Saccharomyces\nList-II\n(I) Unicellular sac fungus\n(II) Bread mould\n(III) Source of an antibiotic\n(IV) Parasite on mustard\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Rhizopus is the bread mould and Albugo parasitises mustard. Penicillium is a source of antibiotics, and Saccharomyces (yeast) is the rare unicellular ascomycete.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following diseases are caused by viruses?\nMumps, Typhoid, Herpes, Cholera, Influenza, Small pox, Tetanus, AIDS","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Mumps, herpes, influenza, small pox and AIDS are viral diseases, which makes 5. Typhoid, cholera and tetanus are bacterial diseases.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (feature) with List-II (protist group):\nList-I\nA. Protein-rich pellicle\nB. Plasmodium spreading over several feet\nC. Walls embedded with silica, fitting like a soap box\nD. Stiff cellulose plates on the outer wall surface\nList-II\nI. Dinoflagellates\nII. Slime moulds\nIII. Euglenoids\nIV. Diatoms","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The pellicle belongs to euglenoids and the plasmodium to slime moulds. Silica soap-box walls are found in diatoms, and cellulose plates in dinoflagellates. The trap is swapping the diatom and dinoflagellate wall types.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Methanogens are archaebacteria present in the gut of several ruminant animals such as cows and buffaloes.\nStatement II: Methanogens are responsible for the production of methane (biogas) from the dung of these animals.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Methanogens live in marshy areas and also in the gut of ruminants. Their activity is the reason methane (biogas) can be produced from the dung of cattle and buffaloes, so both statements are correct.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"A culture of Euglena that was growing in sunlight is shifted to complete darkness. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?","image":"","options":["The cells die at once because they are obligate photoautotrophs","The cells form a plasmodium and then fruiting bodies","The cells behave like heterotrophs by predating on smaller organisms","The cells develop a cellulose wall and enter dormancy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Euglena photosynthesises in sunlight. When deprived of light, it switches to heterotrophic nutrition by predating on smaller organisms, which shows its mixotrophic nature. Forming a plasmodium and fruiting bodies is a slime mould feature.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes chemosynthetic autotrophic bacteria?","image":"","options":["They trap light energy using chlorophyll a and recycle carbon only","They oxidise inorganic substances such as ammonia and nitrites and use the released energy for ATP production, playing a role in recycling nitrogen, phosphorus, iron and sulphur","They depend on dead organic matter for energy and are the chief decomposers in nature","They live only in the gut of ruminants and produce methane from dung"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Chemosynthetic autotrophs get energy by oxidising inorganic compounds, not from light, and they help recycle nutrients like N, P, Fe and S. Decomposition of organic matter is a feature of heterotrophic bacteria, while methane production in ruminant guts is done by methanogens, which are archaebacteria.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Viroids are infectious agents smaller than viruses.\nReason (R): A viroid consists of free DNA of low molecular weight that lacks a protein coat.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Diener's viroid, which causes potato spindle tuber disease, is smaller than viruses, so A is true. However, it is a free RNA, not DNA, so R is false. Students often get the protein-coat part right but miss the nucleic acid type.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (organism) with List-II (feature):\nList-I:\nA. Nostoc\nB. Gonyaulax\nC. Mycoplasma\nD. Euglena\nList-II:\nI. Causes red tides\nII. Fixes nitrogen in heterocysts\nIII. Pellicle and two unequal flagella\nIV. Completely lacks a cell wall","image":"","options":["A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Nostoc fixes nitrogen in heterocysts, and Gonyaulax causes red tides. Mycoplasma has no cell wall at all, and Euglena has a pellicle with one short and one long flagellum.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Diatoms have left behind huge deposits of their cell walls in their habitats over billions of years.\nReason (R): Diatom cell walls are embedded with silica, which makes them indestructible.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The silica-embedded walls do not decompose. They therefore accumulate as diatomaceous earth, so R directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"In a lichen, the partner that is autotrophic and prepares food for the association is called the:","image":"","options":["Mycobiont","Phycobiont","Mycorrhiza","Capsomere"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The algal partner (phycobiont) is autotrophic and makes food. The fungal partner (mycobiont) is heterotrophic and provides shelter, water and minerals. Mycorrhiza is a fungus–root association, not a lichen component.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about Kingdom Animalia is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Animals depend directly or indirectly on plants for food","Higher animal forms show elaborate sensory and neuromotor mechanisms","All members of the kingdom are capable of locomotion","Sexual reproduction is by copulation of male and female, followed by embryological development"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT says that most animals, not all, are capable of locomotion. The other three statements match the text directly.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\nA. Amoeba\nB. Trypanosoma\nC. Paramoecium\nD. Plasmodium\nList-II:\nI. Cavity (gullet) into which coordinated rows of cilia steer food-laden water\nII. Captures prey by putting out pseudopodia\nIII. Infectious spore-like stage in the life cycle\nIV. Bears flagella; parasitic forms cause sleeping sickness\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Amoeba is amoeboid and uses pseudopodia. Trypanosoma is a flagellate that causes sleeping sickness. Paramoecium is a ciliate with a gullet, and Plasmodium is a sporozoan with an infectious spore-like stage.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In ascomycetes and basidiomycetes, fusion of two haploid cells is immediately followed by karyogamy, so diploid cells form at once.\nStatement II: In basidiomycetes, karyogamy and meiosis take place in the basidium, producing four basidiospores.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"In ascomycetes and basidiomycetes, a dikaryotic (n + n) phase comes between plasmogamy and karyogamy, so Statement I is wrong. Statement II correctly describes karyogamy and meiosis in the basidium, which yield four basidiospores.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In Phycomycetes, a zygospore is formed by the fusion of two gametes.\nStatement II: The gametes of all phycomycetes are always similar in morphology (isogamous).\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A zygospore does form by the fusion of two gametes, so Statement I is correct. Statement II is wrong because the gametes may be isogamous, anisogamous or oogamous.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"The three-domain system leads to a six-kingdom classification. It does this by splitting which kingdom of Whittaker's system into two domains?","image":"","options":["Protista","Fungi","Monera","Plantae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The three-domain system splits the prokaryotic Kingdom Monera into two domains. All the eukaryotic kingdoms are kept together in the third domain. Protista is a common trap, but it is eukaryotic.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"A diagram shows a virus with a polyhedral head, a short collar, a contractile sheath and tail fibres at its base. Which statement about this virus is correct?","image":"","options":["It infects plants and usually has single-stranded RNA","It infects animals and always has single-stranded RNA","It infects bacteria and usually has double-stranded DNA","It infects bacteria and contains both DNA and RNA"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The head–collar–sheath–tail fibre structure is a bacteriophage, which infects bacteria and usually has double-stranded DNA. No virus contains both DNA and RNA.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about Kingdom Fungi is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Fungi living with the roots of higher plants form mycorrhiza, while those living with algae form lichens","Vegetative reproduction in fungi can occur by fragmentation, fission and budding","The cell formed after karyogamy is haploid and directly gives rise to spores by mitosis","Fungi that absorb soluble organic matter from dead substrates are called saprophytes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Karyogamy fuses two haploid nuclei, so the resulting zygote is diploid. Haploid spores arise from it by meiosis, not mitosis. The other three statements match NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following diseases are caused by viruses?\nMumps, Typhoid, Herpes, Citrus canker, Influenza, Creutzfeldt–Jacob disease, AIDS, Tetanus","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Four of these are viral: mumps, herpes, influenza and AIDS. Typhoid, citrus canker and tetanus are bacterial. CJD is caused by a prion, which is the main trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following groups is NOT included in Kingdom Plantae under the five kingdom classification?","image":"","options":["Algae","Bryophytes","Fungi","Gymnosperms"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Plantae includes algae, bryophytes, pteridophytes, gymnosperms and angiosperms. Fungi are heterotrophic, have chitinous walls and form a separate kingdom.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, which feature of archaebacteria is responsible for their survival in extreme conditions?","image":"","options":["Presence of chlorophyll a","Presence of heterocysts","Complete absence of a cell wall","A cell wall structure different from that of other bacteria"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Archaebacteria differ from other bacteria in their cell wall structure, and this feature lets them survive harsh habitats. Complete absence of a cell wall describes Mycoplasma, not archaebacteria.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which option gives the correct sequence of events in the sexual cycle of a mushroom such as Agaricus?","image":"","options":["Fusion of somatic cells → dikaryotic stage → basidium formation → karyogamy → meiosis → four exogenous basidiospores","Fusion of gametes from sex organs → diploid zygospore → meiosis → endogenous spores in sporangium","Fusion of somatic cells → karyogamy → dikaryotic stage → meiosis → four endogenous basidiospores","Basidium formation → fusion of somatic cells → meiosis → karyogamy → four exogenous basidiospores"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Plasmogamy of somatic cells produces a dikaryotic structure, which forms the basidium. Karyogamy and then meiosis take place in the basidium, giving four exogenous basidiospores. The other options misplace the dikaryotic stage, wrongly include sex organs, or reverse karyogamy and meiosis.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the classes of fungi is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Phycomycetes have aseptate, coenocytic mycelium, and their asexual spores are produced endogenously in a sporangium","In ascomycetes, conidia are produced exogenously on special hyphae called conidiophores","In basidiomycetes, basidiospores are produced endogenously inside the basidium","Deuteromycetes reproduce only by asexual spores called conidia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Basidiospores are produced exogenously on the basidium. The endogenous production of sexual spores in a sac (ascus) is a feature of ascomycetes, which is the trap here.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (fungal class) with List-II (characteristic reproductive feature).\nList-I:\nA. Phycomycetes\nB. Ascomycetes\nC. Basidiomycetes\nD. Deuteromycetes\nList-II:\nI. Sexual spores produced exogenously, four on each club-like structure\nII. Reproduce only by asexual conidia\nIII. Zygospore formed by fusion of two gametes\nIV. Sexual spores produced endogenously in sac-like structures","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Phycomycetes form zygospores. Ascomycetes produce ascospores endogenously in asci. Basidiomycetes bear four basidiospores exogenously on a basidium. Deuteromycetes reproduce only by conidia. The common trap is mixing up endogenous asci with exogenous basidia.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Compared to many other organisms, bacteria as a group show the most extensive metabolic diversity.\n(b) Chemosynthetic autotrophic bacteria use light energy to synthesise their food from inorganic substrates.\n(c) Archaebacteria differ from other bacteria in cell wall structure, and this feature is responsible for their survival in extreme conditions.\n(d) Methanogens are present in the gut of ruminants and help produce biogas from their dung.\n(e) Thermoacidophiles are archaebacteria characteristically found in marshy areas.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (c) and (d) are correct. Statement (b) is wrong because chemosynthetic autotrophs get their energy by oxidising inorganic substances, not from light. Statement (e) is wrong because thermoacidophiles live in hot springs, while methanogens occupy marshy areas.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Sexual reproduction in members of Kingdom Protista involves:","image":"","options":["Fission followed by spore formation","Cell fusion and zygote formation","Plasmogamy followed by a long dikaryophase","Transfer of DNA through a conjugation tube only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Protists reproduce sexually by a process involving cell fusion and zygote formation. A prolonged dikaryophase is characteristic of ascomycete and basidiomycete fungi, not protists.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Albugo, the fungus that causes white spots on mustard leaves, belongs to which class of fungi?","image":"","options":["Ascomycetes","Basidiomycetes","Phycomycetes","Deuteromycetes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists Albugo, the parasitic fungus on mustard, as a phycomycete along with Mucor and Rhizopus. Rusts and smuts are the parasitic examples given for basidiomycetes, not Albugo.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (organism) with List-II (effect caused):\nList-I\nA. Trypanosoma\nB. Plasmodium\nC. Puccinia\nD. Albugo\nList-II\nI. White spots on mustard leaves\nII. Malaria\nIII. Sleeping sickness\nIV. Wheat rust\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Trypanosoma is a flagellated protozoan that causes sleeping sickness, and Plasmodium is the sporozoan that causes malaria. Puccinia causes wheat rust, and Albugo is the parasitic phycomycete on mustard.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the five kingdom classification, Chlamydomonas and Amoeba are placed in the same kingdom, although earlier systems placed them in different kingdoms.\nReason (R): Both Chlamydomonas and Amoeba possess a cell wall.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Both organisms are unicellular eukaryotes and so belong to Protista, which makes A true. R is false because Amoeba lacks a cell wall; that is why it was earlier placed with animals, while walled Chlamydomonas was placed with algae.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about viruses is correct?","image":"","options":["Viruses that infect plants generally have double-stranded DNA as their genetic material","W.M. Stanley showed that crystallised viruses consist largely of nucleic acids","Some viruses contain both RNA and DNA in the same particle","Bacteriophages are usually double-stranded DNA viruses"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, bacteriophages usually carry double-stranded DNA. Plant viruses generally have single-stranded RNA, and Stanley's virus crystals were mostly protein. No virus has both RNA and DNA.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Viroids cause potato spindle tuber disease.\nReason (R): The RNA of a viroid is of low molecular weight.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Diener found that a free, low-molecular-weight RNA lacking a protein coat causes potato spindle tuber disease, so both statements are true. However, the low molecular weight of the RNA does not explain why the viroid causes this particular disease.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following organisms are protozoans?\nAmoeba, Euglena, Gonyaulax, Trypanosoma, Plasmodium, Paramoecium, slime mould, Entamoeba","image":"","options":["4","6","3","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The protozoans are Amoeba, Trypanosoma, Plasmodium, Paramoecium and Entamoeba, which makes five. Euglena is a euglenoid, Gonyaulax is a dinoflagellate and slime moulds are a separate saprophytic group; these are protists but not protozoans.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"A microscope slide shows a marine, photosynthetic unicellular organism. Its outer surface is covered with stiff cellulose plates. It has two flagella: one runs lengthwise and the other lies crosswise in a groove between the plates. To which group does it belong?","image":"","options":["Euglenoids","Chrysophytes","Dinoflagellates","Ciliated protozoans"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Dinoflagellates are the protists with stiff cellulose wall plates and two flagella, one longitudinal and one transverse in a furrow. Euglenoids have a flexible protein-rich pellicle instead of a wall, and their two flagella differ in length (one short, one long).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about Kingdom Protista is correct?","image":"","options":["It includes all unicellular organisms, whether prokaryotic or eukaryotic","Its members lack membrane-bound organelles because they are single-celled","It groups together Chlamydomonas, which has a cell wall, and Paramoecium, which lacks one, although they were earlier placed in different kingdoms","Its members are primarily terrestrial and reproduce only asexually"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Protista unites single-celled eukaryotes such as Chlamydomonas (walled, formerly an alga) and Paramoecium (wall-less, formerly an animal). Prokaryotes belong to Monera. Protists do have membrane-bound organelles, are primarily aquatic, and reproduce both asexually and sexually.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"A cavity called the gullet, which opens to the outside of the cell surface, is a characteristic feature of","image":"","options":["Paramoecium","Amoeba","Trypanosoma","Plasmodium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Ciliated protozoans such as Paramoecium have a gullet. Coordinated beating of cilia drives food-laden water into it.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Agaricus\n(B) Ustilago\n(C) Puccinia\n(D) Trichoderma\nList-II\n(I) Rust fungus\n(II) Imperfect fungus\n(III) Mushroom\n(IV) Smut\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Agaricus is a mushroom, Ustilago causes smut, Puccinia is the rust fungus and Trichoderma is a deuteromycete (imperfect fungus). The common trap is swapping the smut and rust genera.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which group of protozoans moves and captures prey by putting out pseudopodia (false feet)?","image":"","options":["Flagellated protozoans","Amoeboid protozoans","Ciliated protozoans","Sporozoans"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Amoeboid protozoans such as Amoeba extend pseudopodia both to move and to engulf prey. Ciliates use cilia and flagellates use flagella instead.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Methanogens in the gut of ruminants such as cows and buffaloes produce methane (biogas) from their dung.\nStatement II: Archaebacteria have a cell wall structure different from other bacteria, and this helps them survive in extreme conditions.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that methanogens in the ruminant gut produce methane from dung. It also states that the distinct cell wall of archaebacteria is responsible for their survival in harsh habitats. So both statements are correct.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (fungus) with List-II (feature/importance):\nList-I\nA. Yeast\nB. Puccinia\nC. Penicillium\nD. Albugo\nList-II\nI. Source of antibiotics\nII. Parasite on mustard\nIII. Used in making bread and beer\nIV. Causes wheat rust\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The correct pairs are:\n- Yeast is used in bread and beer making.\n- Puccinia causes wheat rust.\n- Penicillium is a source of antibiotics.\n- Albugo is the parasitic fungus on mustard.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following events in the life of a slime mould in the correct order:\nP. Plasmodium differentiates to form fruiting bodies\nQ. Walled spores are dispersed by air currents\nR. Cells aggregate to form a plasmodium under suitable conditions\nS. Body moves along decaying twigs and leaves, engulfing organic material","image":"","options":["R → S → Q → P","S → R → P → Q","S → P → R → Q","R → P → S → Q"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The slime mould first feeds saprophytically on decaying matter and then aggregates into a plasmodium under suitable conditions. When conditions become unfavourable, the plasmodium forms fruiting bodies, and their spores are dispersed by air. The common trap is placing fruiting-body formation before plasmodium formation.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Fungal hyphae that are continuous tubes without cross walls and are filled with multinucleated cytoplasm are called:","image":"","options":["Septate hyphae","Coenocytic hyphae","Dikaryotic hyphae","Conidiophores"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Coenocytic hyphae have no septa, so their cytoplasm is continuous and multinucleate. Septate hyphae have cross walls. Dikaryotic hyphae refers to cells that each contain two nuclei.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about protists is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Euglenoids have a protein-rich pellicle instead of a cell wall, which makes the body flexible","The spores formed by slime moulds lack true walls and are therefore short-lived","In most dinoflagellates, one flagellum lies longitudinally and the other transversely in a furrow between the wall plates","Rapid multiplication of Gonyaulax can make the sea appear red"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Slime mould spores possess true walls. They are extremely resistant and can survive for many years. The other three statements agree with NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"The cell walls of fungi are mainly composed of:","image":"","options":["Cellulose and pectin","Chitin and polysaccharides","Silica embedded in two overlapping shells","Polysaccharides and amino acids (peptidoglycan-like)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The fungal cell wall is made of chitin and polysaccharides. This wall composition was one reason fungi were separated from green plants, which have cellulosic walls. Silica walls belong to diatoms, and polysaccharide plus amino acid walls belong to Monera.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\nA. Ivanowsky (1892)\nB. Beijerinck (1898)\nC. Stanley (1935)\nD. Diener (1971)\nList-II:\nI. Showed viruses can be crystallised and the crystals are largely protein\nII. Discovered a coat-less infectious RNA causing potato spindle tuber disease\nIII. Found that the agent of tobacco mosaic disease passes through bacteria-proof filters\nIV. Showed sap of infected plants infects healthy plants and named it Contagium vivum fluidum\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Ivanowsky found that the tobacco mosaic agent is smaller than bacteria and passes through bacteria-proof filters. Beijerinck named the infectious fluid, Stanley crystallised viruses, and Diener discovered viroids. Students often confuse Ivanowsky's and Beijerinck's contributions.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Compared to many other organisms, bacteria as a group show the most extensive metabolic diversity.\nStatement II: All autotrophic bacteria synthesise their food by photosynthesis.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Bacteria indeed show the widest metabolic diversity, so Statement I is correct. Statement II is wrong because autotrophic bacteria may be either photosynthetic or chemosynthetic.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"A unicellular, wall-less eukaryote is observed in pond water. Based on NCERT, which one of the following observations would rule out placing it among the protozoans?","image":"","options":["It extends pseudopodia to engulf smaller organisms","It carries out photosynthesis when exposed to sunlight","It has an infectious spore-like stage in its life cycle","It lives as a parasite inside another organism"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"All protozoans are heterotrophs, so a photosynthetic ability excludes the organism from this group. It instead suggests a euglenoid such as Euglena, which is photosynthetic in light and predatory in darkness. The other three features all occur in protozoan groups.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"A symbiotic association between a fungus and the roots of higher plants is known as:","image":"","options":["Lichen","Mycorrhiza","Mycelium","Plasmodium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Fungi associated with the roots of higher plants form mycorrhiza. A lichen is a fungus associated with algae, not roots. Mycelium is simply the network of hyphae.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"In a lichen, the algal partner is called the ______ and its mode of nutrition is ______.","image":"","options":["mycobiont; heterotrophic","phycobiont; autotrophic","phycobiont; heterotrophic","mycobiont; autotrophic"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The algal component of a lichen is the phycobiont. It is autotrophic and prepares food for the fungus. The fungal component is the heterotrophic mycobiont.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"A bacterium seen under the microscope is shaped like a comma. Into which shape category would it be placed?","image":"","options":["Coccus","Bacillus","Vibrio","Spirillum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Comma-shaped bacteria are vibrio. Coccus is spherical, bacillus is rod-shaped and spirillum is spiral. Do not confuse the curved comma form with the fully coiled spiral form.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Associations between Mycobiont and Phycobiont are found in","image":"","options":["Mycorrhiza","Root","Lichens","BGA"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Lichens are symbiotic associations between an algal partner (phycobiont) and a fungal partner (mycobiont).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 2 (MCQ Q8)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following organisms completely lacks a cell wall?","image":"","options":["Anabaena","Nostoc","Mycoplasma","Methanogens"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Mycoplasma are the only organisms in this list that completely lack a cell wall. Cyanobacteria such as Anabaena and Nostoc are eubacteria with a rigid wall, and methanogens are archaebacteria that also have a wall, though with a different structure.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about protozoans is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Marine amoeboid protozoans bear silica shells on their surface","In ciliated protozoans, coordinated beating of rows of cilia steers water laden with food into the gullet","Sporozoans move actively with thousands of cilia and have an infectious spore-like stage","Parasitic flagellated protozoans can cause diseases such as sleeping sickness"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Sporozoans are defined by an infectious spore-like stage in their life cycle. Bearing thousands of cilia is a feature of ciliates such as Paramoecium, not sporozoans.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following gives the correct sequence of events in the sexual cycle of fungi?","image":"","options":["Karyogamy → Plasmogamy → Meiosis in zygote","Meiosis → Plasmogamy → Karyogamy","Plasmogamy → Karyogamy → Meiosis in zygote","Plasmogamy → Meiosis → Karyogamy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"First the protoplasms of two gametes fuse (plasmogamy), then the nuclei fuse (karyogamy). Finally, meiosis in the diploid zygote produces haploid spores.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"The cell walls of diatoms are embedded with which substance, which makes them practically indestructible?","image":"","options":["Cellulose","Silica","Chitin","Calcium carbonate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Diatom walls are embedded with silica, so they do not decay and accumulate over time. Cellulose plates are found in dinoflagellates, and chitin is found in fungi.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Slime moulds move along decaying twigs and leaves, engulfing organic material. Their mode of nutrition is best described as:","image":"","options":["Photosynthetic autotrophic","Chemosynthetic autotrophic","Saprophytic","Parasitic"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT describes slime moulds as saprophytic protists that feed on decaying organic matter. They have no photosynthetic or chemosynthetic ability.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"The infectious agent discovered by T.O. Diener in 1971, which is smaller than viruses, causes which disease?","image":"","options":["Tobacco mosaic disease","Citrus canker","Potato spindle tuber disease","Bovine spongiform encephalopathy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Diener's viroid caused potato spindle tuber disease. Tobacco mosaic is caused by a virus, citrus canker by a bacterium and BSE by a prion.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following features are correct for Deuteromycetes?\n(a) Mycelium is septate and branched\n(b) Reproduction occurs only by asexual spores called conidia\n(c) A large number are decomposers of litter and help in mineral cycling\n(d) Sexual spores are produced in ascocarps\n(e) Mycelium is aseptate and coenocytic","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Features (a), (b) and (c) are correct. Ascocarps belong to ascomycetes, and an aseptate coenocytic mycelium is typical of phycomycetes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"What character did Aristotle use to divide animals into two groups?","image":"","options":["Presence or absence of a backbone","Ability or inability to fly","Presence or absence of red blood","Aquatic or terrestrial habitat"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Aristotle separated animals into those that had red blood and those that did not. Vertebrate/invertebrate grouping by backbone is a later idea.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Earlier systems grouped bacteria, blue-green algae, fungi, mosses, ferns, gymnosperms and angiosperms together as 'Plants' because all had a cell wall. Which pair from this old grouping was split into different Whittaker kingdoms solely because one member is unicellular and the other multicellular? (Both members are eukaryotic, photosynthetic and walled.)","image":"","options":["Nostoc and Spirogyra","Mucor and a fern","Chlamydomonas and Spirogyra","Chlorella and Chlamydomonas"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Chlamydomonas (unicellular, now in Protista) and Spirogyra (multicellular alga, in Plantae) differ only in cellularity. Nostoc and Spirogyra are separated by prokaryotic versus eukaryotic structure. Mucor and a fern differ in wall composition and nutrition. Chlorella and Chlamydomonas both fall in Protista.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"In a fungus, the long, slender thread-like structures that make up the body are collectively organised into a network. What is this network called?","image":"","options":["Hypha","Mycelium","Plasmodium","Pellicle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Each thread is a hypha, and the network of hyphae is called the mycelium. Plasmodium is the aggregation formed by slime moulds, and the pellicle is the protein-rich layer of euglenoids.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"In basidiomycetes, karyogamy and meiosis take place in which structure?","image":"","options":["Ascus","Basidium","Sporangium","Conidiophore"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In basidiomycetes, the dikaryotic structure gives rise to a basidium, where nuclear fusion (karyogamy) and then meiosis occur. Asci serve this role in ascomycetes, not basidiomycetes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Naked cytoplasm, multinucleated and saprophytic are the characteristics of","image":"","options":["Monera","Protista","Fungi","Slime molds"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Slime moulds are saprophytic protists. Under suitable conditions they form a plasmodium, a multinucleate mass of naked protoplasm without a cell wall.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 2 (MCQ Q4)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Diatoms have left behind huge deposits of their cell walls over billions of years, forming 'diatomaceous earth'. Which property of their cell wall makes this possible?","image":"","options":["The walls are made of chitin, which resists microbial digestion","The walls are embedded with silica, which makes them practically indestructible","The walls consist of stiff cellulose plates that are shed regularly","The walls are replaced by a protein-rich pellicle that fossilises easily"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Diatom walls form two thin overlapping shells, like a soap box, and are embedded with silica, so they do not decay and accumulate as diatomaceous earth. Stiff cellulose plates are a feature of dinoflagellates, and the pellicle belongs to euglenoids.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"In Euglena, the cell wall is absent. Which structure covers the body instead?","image":"","options":["A cellulose wall made of stiff plates","A protein-rich pellicle","A silica shell of two overlapping halves","A chitinous wall"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Euglenoids have no cell wall. They have a protein-rich layer called the pellicle, which keeps the body flexible. Stiff cellulose plates belong to dinoflagellates and silica shells belong to diatoms.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"The cell wall of fungi is mainly composed of:","image":"","options":["Cellulose","Peptidoglycan (polysaccharide + amino acid)","Chitin and polysaccharides","Silica embedded plates"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Fungal walls are made of chitin and polysaccharides. Cellulose walls are characteristic of plants, and a polysaccharide plus amino acid wall is characteristic of Monera. Silica-embedded walls belong to diatoms.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In every fungus, the fusion of two compatible haploid cells immediately results in a diploid cell.\nStatement II: Fungi form fruiting bodies in which reduction division occurs, leading to the formation of haploid spores.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Only some fungi become diploid immediately after fusion. Ascomycetes and basidiomycetes pass through a dikaryotic (n + n) phase first, so Statement I is wrong. Statement II is correct because meiosis occurs in fruiting bodies to produce haploid spores.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"A diagram shows a virus with a polyhedral head, a collar, a sheath and tail fibres. It is being compared with a rod-shaped virus that causes mosaic disease in tobacco. Which statement correctly describes the first virus?","image":"","options":["It is a plant virus with single-stranded RNA wound inside a helical capsid","It infects bacteria and usually carries double-stranded DNA protected by a capsid made of capsomeres","It is an infectious agent made only of abnormally folded protein","It is a free RNA molecule without any protein coat"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A head, collar, sheath and tail fibres identify a bacteriophage, which is usually a double-stranded DNA virus. Its nucleic acid is protected by a capsid built from capsomeres. Option 1 describes TMV, option 3 a prion and option 4 a viroid.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the flagella of a typical euglenoid?","image":"","options":["One flagellum lying in a transverse furrow","Two flagella of equal length","Two flagella, one short and one long","Thousands of short flagella arranged in rows"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Euglenoids bear two unequal flagella, one short and one long. Equal-length flagella are not described for them. Flagella lying in furrows between wall plates are a dinoflagellate feature.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which feature correctly describes Mycoplasma?","image":"","options":["They have a rigid cell wall and a single flagellum","They are the largest prokaryotic cells and are strictly aerobic","They completely lack a cell wall and are the smallest living cells known","They are photosynthetic and contain chlorophyll a"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Mycoplasma completely lack a cell wall. They are the smallest living cells known and can survive without oxygen. A rigid wall is typical of eubacteria, not Mycoplasma.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about spore formation in fungi is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["In ascomycetes, conidia are produced exogenously on conidiophores","In ascomycetes, ascospores are produced endogenously in asci","In basidiomycetes, basidiospores are produced endogenously inside the basidium","In phycomycetes, zoospores and aplanospores are produced endogenously in a sporangium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Basidiospores are borne exogenously on the basidium, four per basidium. This contrasts with ascospores, which form endogenously inside asci.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Kingdom) with List-II (Body organisation as given in the five kingdom comparison).\nList-I:\n(A) Monera\n(B) Fungi\n(C) Plantae\n(D) Animalia\nList-II:\n(I) Tissue/organ\n(II) Cellular\n(III) Tissue/organ/organ system\n(IV) Multicellular/loose tissue","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Monera are cellular, and fungi are multicellular with loose tissue. Plants reach the tissue/organ level, while only animals show tissue/organ/organ system organisation. The trap is swapping the plant and animal levels.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following spore types are produced during asexual reproduction in fungi?\nConidia, Oospores, Sporangiospores, Ascospores, Zoospores, Basidiospores","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The asexual spores are conidia, sporangiospores and zoospores, which makes three. Oospores, ascospores and basidiospores are sexual spores.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about acellular infectious agents:\nI. Viruses infecting plants generally have single-stranded RNA, whereas bacteriophages are usually double-stranded DNA viruses.\nII. The viroid causing potato spindle tuber disease is a free RNA of high molecular weight enclosed in a capsid.\nIII. Prions are abnormally folded proteins, similar in size to viruses, that cause certain neurological diseases.\nIV. Some animal viruses contain both DNA and RNA as genetic material.\nWhich of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["I and II only","I and III only","II, III and IV only","I, III and IV only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements I and III agree with NCERT. Statement II is wrong because a viroid lacks a protein coat and its RNA is of low molecular weight. Statement IV is wrong because no virus contains both RNA and DNA.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are spores produced by sexual reproduction in fungi?\nConidia, Oospores, Ascospores, Sporangiospores, Basidiospores, Zoospores","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Oospores, ascospores and basidiospores are sexual spores. Conidia, sporangiospores and zoospores are asexual spores, which is the usual trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Entamoeba is an amoeboid protozoan that lives as a parasite.\nStatement II: All flagellated protozoans are parasitic.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT names Entamoeba as a parasitic amoeboid form, so Statement I is correct. Flagellated protozoans may be either free-living or parasitic, so Statement II is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Lichens are generally not found growing in areas with heavy air pollution.\nReason (R): In a lichen, the mycobiont prepares food for the phycobiont, while the phycobiont absorbs water and minerals.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Lichens do not grow in polluted areas, which is why they are good pollution indicators, so A is true. R reverses the roles: the alga (phycobiont) makes the food, and the fungus (mycobiont) provides shelter and absorbs water and minerals.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are characteristic features of Kingdom Animalia as described in NCERT?\n(i) Multicellular organisation\n(ii) Cells lack cell walls\n(iii) Food is digested in an internal cavity\n(iv) A definite growth pattern leading to adults of definite shape and size\n(v) Alternation of haploid and diploid generations in the life cycle\n(vi) Food reserves stored as starch","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Features (i) to (iv) describe animals. Alternation of generations and starch storage are features of plants, not animals.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"A prokaryote is isolated from the rumen of a buffalo. It produces a combustible gas from ingested plant material. Which of the following best describes this organism?","image":"","options":["A cyanobacterium that fixes nitrogen in heterocysts and releases the gas as a by-product","A thermoacidophile whose acid tolerance allows it to live in the rumen","A chemosynthetic autotroph that oxidises nitrites to release the gas","A methanogen, an archaebacterium with a cell wall structure distinct from eubacteria, also found in marshy areas"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Methane-producing prokaryotes in the gut of ruminants are methanogens. Methanogens are archaebacteria with a distinctive cell wall, and they also live in marshes. Thermoacidophiles are linked to hot springs, and cyanobacteria and chemosynthetic bacteria are eubacteria that do not make biogas.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"The mycelium of members of Phycomycetes is best described as:","image":"","options":["Septate and uninucleate","Aseptate and coenocytic","Septate and dikaryotic","Branched and septate with clamp connections"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Phycomycetes have hyphae without cross walls, forming continuous multinucleate tubes (coenocytic). Septate mycelium is found in ascomycetes, basidiomycetes and deuteromycetes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In basidiomycetes, basidiospores are produced endogenously inside the basidium.\nStatement II: In basidiomycetes, asexual spores are generally not found, but vegetative reproduction by fragmentation is common.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Basidiospores are formed exogenously on the basidium, so Statement I is wrong. Endogenous formation describes ascospores in asci. Statement II correctly describes reproduction in basidiomycetes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Contagium vivum fluidum was proposed by","image":"","options":["D.J. Ivanowsky","M.W. Beijerinck","Stanley","Robert Hooke"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Beijerinck showed that the extract of infected tobacco plants could transmit the disease. He called this infectious fluid 'contagium vivum fluidum'.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 2 (MCQ Q7)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Aristotle made one of the earliest attempts at a scientific classification of plants. Into which groups did he divide them?","image":"","options":["Algae, fungi and mosses","Trees, shrubs and herbs","Monocots and dicots","Flowering and non-flowering plants"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Aristotle used simple morphological characters to group plants into trees, shrubs and herbs. The other groupings belong to much later systems.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (fungus) with List-II (class):\nList-I:\nA. Albugo\nB. Neurospora\nC. Ustilago\nD. Trichoderma\nList-II:\nI. Deuteromycetes\nII. Basidiomycetes\nIII. Phycomycetes\nIV. Ascomycetes","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Albugo (parasite on mustard) is a phycomycete, Neurospora is an ascomycete and Ustilago (smut) is a basidiomycete. Trichoderma is a deuteromycete (imperfect fungus).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In basidiomycetes such as Agaricus, karyogamy and meiosis take place in the basidium, producing four basidiospores.\nStatement II: In basidiomycetes, the basidiospores are produced endogenously inside the basidium, and the basidia are arranged in fruiting bodies called basidiocarps.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Karyogamy and meiosis in the basidium produce four basidiospores, so Statement I is correct. Statement II is wrong because basidiospores are borne exogenously on the basidium. Endogenous spore formation in sacs (asci) is a feature of ascomycetes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are members of Ascomycetes?\nPenicillium, Mucor, Aspergillus, Albugo, Claviceps, Agaricus, Neurospora, yeast","image":"","options":["4","6","5","3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Penicillium, Aspergillus, Claviceps, Neurospora and yeast are ascomycetes, which makes five. Mucor and Albugo are phycomycetes, and Agaricus is a basidiomycete.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"With respect to mode of nutrition, all protozoans are","image":"","options":["Photosynthetic autotrophs","Chemosynthetic autotrophs","Photosynthetic in light and heterotrophic in darkness","Heterotrophs living as predators or parasites"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT states that all protozoans are heterotrophs and live as predators or parasites. Switching between photosynthesis and heterotrophy describes euglenoids, not protozoans.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Halophiles\nB. Thermoacidophiles\nC. Methanogens\nD. Vibrio\nList-II\nI. Comma-shaped bacteria\nII. Hot springs\nIII. Marshy areas\nIV. Extremely salty areas\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Halophiles live in extremely salty areas, thermoacidophiles in hot springs and methanogens in marshy areas. Vibrio is the comma-shaped form of bacteria. A common trap is swapping the habitats of halophiles and thermoacidophiles.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Bacteria show great metabolic diversity. Which nutritional group includes the vast majority of bacteria?","image":"","options":["Photosynthetic autotrophs","Heterotrophs","Chemosynthetic autotrophs","Organisms that switch between autotrophy and heterotrophy depending on light"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Some bacteria are photosynthetic or chemosynthetic autotrophs, but the vast majority are heterotrophs. They depend on other organisms or on dead organic matter for food. The light-dependent switching in the last option describes euglenoids, which are protists, not bacteria.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about Kingdom Fungi is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Coenocytic hyphae have cross walls that divide the cytoplasm into uninucleate compartments","Except for unicellular forms such as yeast, fungi have a filamentous body made of hyphae","In ascomycetes, the nuclear condition during the sexual cycle changes in the order n → n + n → 2n → n","Reduction division in fungi occurs in fruiting bodies, leading to the formation of haploid spores"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Coenocytic hyphae are continuous tubes filled with multinucleated cytoplasm and have no septa. The cross walls described in option 1 are a feature of septate hyphae. The other statements correctly describe the filamentous body, the dikaryotic sequence in ascomycetes, and meiosis in fruiting bodies.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A viroid differs from a virus in lacking a protein coat, and its RNA is of low molecular weight.\nStatement II: Prions, like viroids, are free nucleic acid molecules without a protein coat, and they cause neurological diseases such as BSE.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: a viroid is free, low molecular weight RNA with no protein coat. Statement II is wrong because a prion has no nucleic acid at all; it is an abnormally folded protein, about the size of a virus. The disease part (BSE) is true, but the nature of the agent is described wrongly.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The two kingdom system of Linnaeus' time did not distinguish between prokaryotes and eukaryotes, or between unicellular and multicellular organisms.\nStatement II: The two kingdom system kept the photosynthetic green algae and the non-photosynthetic fungi in separate kingdoms.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The two kingdom system failed to distinguish prokaryotes from eukaryotes and unicellular from multicellular organisms. It also lumped photosynthetic green algae and non-photosynthetic fungi together under plants, so Statement II is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about eubacteria is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Cyanobacteria may be unicellular, colonial or filamentous, and their colonies are generally surrounded by a gelatinous sheath","Chemosynthetic autotrophic bacteria help recycle nutrients such as nitrogen, phosphorus, iron and sulphur","Sexual reproduction in bacteria involves the fusion of male and female gametes to form a diploid zygote","Bacteria reproduce mainly by fission and may form spores under unfavourable conditions"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Bacteria show only a sort of sexual reproduction, in which DNA is transferred in a primitive way from one bacterium to another. Gamete fusion and zygote formation do not occur in bacteria; that kind of process is described for protists. The other three statements match NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following was NOT one of the main criteria used by R.H. Whittaker (1969) in his Five Kingdom Classification?","image":"","options":["Cell structure","Mode of nutrition","Phylogenetic relationships","Presence or absence of red blood"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Whittaker's main criteria were cell structure, body organisation, mode of nutrition, reproduction and phylogenetic relationships. Red blood was Aristotle's criterion for grouping animals.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Nostoc\n(B) Mycoplasma\n(C) Chemosynthetic autotrophic bacteria\n(D) Heterotrophic bacteria\nList-II\n(I) Oxidise ammonia, nitrites and nitrates to obtain energy for ATP production\n(II) Used in making curd from milk\n(III) Heterocysts present\n(IV) No cell wall at all\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Nostoc has heterocysts and Mycoplasma lacks a cell wall. Chemosynthetic autotrophs get energy by oxidising inorganic nitrogen compounds, and heterotrophic bacteria help convert milk into curd.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Kingdom Protista brings together Chlamydomonas and Paramoecium, which were placed in different kingdoms in earlier classification systems.\nReason (R): Both Chlamydomonas and Paramoecium lack a cell wall, so they could not have been placed under plants.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: unicellular eukaryotes were grouped in Protista, uniting Chlamydomonas (formerly an alga under plants) with Paramoecium (formerly an animal). R is false because Chlamydomonas has a cell wall; only Paramoecium lacks one.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (protozoan group) with List-II (example):\nList-I\nA. Amoeboid protozoan\nB. Flagellated protozoan\nC. Ciliated protozoan\nD. Sporozoan\nList-II\nI. Plasmodium\nII. Paramoecium\nIII. Entamoeba\nIV. Trypanosoma\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Entamoeba is a parasitic amoeboid form, Trypanosoma is flagellated, Paramoecium is ciliated and Plasmodium is a sporozoan. A common trap is placing Entamoeba with the flagellates.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is a unicellular fungus used in making bread and beer?","image":"","options":["Puccinia","Penicillium","Albugo","Yeast"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Yeasts are the exception among fungi because they are unicellular, and they are used for bread and beer. Puccinia, Penicillium and Albugo are all filamentous.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Viroids are free RNA molecules of low molecular weight that lack a protein coat.\nStatement II: Prions are abnormally folded proteins that are much smaller than viroids.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Viroids are indeed free, low-molecular-weight RNA without a protein coat. Prions are abnormally folded proteins, but NCERT states they are similar in size to viruses, not smaller than viroids.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Members of phycomycetes are found in\ni. Aquatic habitats\nii. On decaying wood\niii. Moist and damp places\niv. As obligate parasites on plants\nChoose from the following options","image":"","options":["None of the above","i and iv","ii and iii","All of the above"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, phycomycetes occur in aquatic habitats, on decaying wood and in moist, damp places. They also occur as obligate parasites on plants, so all four statements are correct.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 2 (MCQ Q12)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The cell walls of diatoms are embedded with silica, so they are practically indestructible.\nStatement II: Diatomaceous earth is gritty and is used in polishing and in the filtration of oils and syrups.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Diatom walls contain silica and resist decay, so they pile up over billions of years as diatomaceous earth. Because this soil is gritty, it is used for polishing and for filtering oils and syrups.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Organisms living in salty areas are called as","image":"","options":["Methanogens","Halophiles","Heliophytes","Thermoacidophiles"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Halophiles are archaebacteria that live in extremely salty environments. Methanogens live in marshy areas, and thermoacidophiles live in hot springs.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 2 (MCQ Q3)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In ascomycetes and basidiomycetes, plasmogamy between two compatible haploid hyphae is immediately followed by karyogamy, giving diploid cells.\nStatement II: In the sexual cycle of fungi, meiosis in the zygote results in haploid spores.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"In ascomycetes and basidiomycetes, a dikaryotic (n + n) phase comes between plasmogamy and karyogamy, so Statement I is wrong. The fusion of haploid cells immediately gives diploid cells only in some other fungi. Statement II correctly describes the final step of the fungal sexual cycle.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Some ascomycetes are described as coprophilous. This term means that they:","image":"","options":["Grow on dung","Grow on decaying wood in damp places","Live as obligate parasites on plants","Live symbiotically with plant roots"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Coprophilous fungi grow on dung. NCERT lists this as one of the modes of life of ascomycetes, along with saprophytic, decomposer and parasitic.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (fungus) with List-II (class):\nList-I\nA. Neurospora\nB. Ustilago\nC. Trichoderma\nD. Mucor\nList-II\nI. Phycomycetes\nII. Deuteromycetes\nIII. Basidiomycetes\nIV. Ascomycetes\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Neurospora is an ascomycete and Ustilago (smut) is a basidiomycete. Trichoderma is a deuteromycete (imperfect fungus), and Mucor is a phycomycete.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Gonyaulax\nB. Trypanosoma\nC. Nostoc\nD. Neurospora\nList-II\nI. Heterocysts\nII. Biochemical and genetic studies\nIII. Red tides\nIV. Sleeping sickness\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Gonyaulax is a red dinoflagellate that causes red tides, and Trypanosoma causes sleeping sickness. Nostoc fixes nitrogen in heterocysts, and Neurospora is used extensively in biochemical and genetic work.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about protozoans are correct?\n(i) Entamoeba is a parasitic amoeboid protozoan.\n(ii) All flagellated protozoans are parasitic.\n(iii) In ciliated protozoans, the gullet opens to the outside of the cell surface.\n(iv) Plasmodium, the malarial parasite, is a flagellated protozoan.\n(v) Amoeboid protozoans may live in fresh water, sea water or moist soil.\n(vi) Some protozoans are photosynthetic, while others are predators or parasites.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (iii) and (v) are correct. Flagellates can be free-living or parasitic, so (ii) is wrong. Plasmodium is a sporozoan, so (iv) is wrong. All protozoans are heterotrophs, so (vi) is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about Euglena is correct?","image":"","options":["Its rigid cellulose cell wall keeps its body shape fixed","It has a protein-rich pellicle and two flagella of unequal length","Its pigments are completely different from those of higher plants","It is a strict autotroph and dies when kept in darkness"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Euglenoids have a flexible, protein-rich pellicle instead of a cell wall, and they bear one short and one long flagellum. Their pigments are identical to those of higher plants, and in darkness they feed heterotrophically by preying on smaller organisms.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): All protozoans obtain their food heterotrophically, either as predators or as parasites.\nReason (R): Protozoans are believed to be primitive relatives of plants.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The assertion is true because every protozoan is heterotrophic. The reason is false: NCERT describes protozoans as primitive relatives of animals, not plants.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Viruses were not given a place in Whittaker's five kingdom classification.\nReason (R): Viruses are non-cellular, and outside a living host cell they exist as an inert crystalline structure.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Both statements are true. Viruses were left out of the five kingdoms because they lack a cell structure and are not considered truly living. Their inert, crystalline nature outside the host is exactly why they were excluded, so R explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"A diagram shows a virus with a polyhedral head, a collar and a sheath below the head, and several thin tail fibres at the base. Which option correctly gives its usual host and the type of genetic material it typically carries?","image":"","options":["Bacteria; double-stranded DNA","Tobacco plants; single-stranded RNA","Animal cells; double-stranded RNA","Bacteria; single-stranded RNA"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A head, collar, sheath and tail fibres are the features of a bacteriophage, which infects bacteria and is usually a dsDNA virus. The trap is TMV, a helical, rod-shaped plant virus with ssRNA.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following diseases is NOT caused by a bacterium?","image":"","options":["Tetanus","Citrus canker","Typhoid","Mumps"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Cholera, typhoid, tetanus and citrus canker are bacterial diseases listed in NCERT. Mumps is caused by a virus. Citrus canker is a common trap because it is a plant disease, but it is still bacterial.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Diatoms are the chief producers in the oceans.\nReason (R): The cell walls of diatoms are embedded with silica, which makes them practically indestructible.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Both statements are true. Diatoms are major producers because they are abundant photosynthetic plankton. Silica in their walls explains diatomaceous earth deposits, not their role as producers.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"A fungus was long known only from its conidia and was therefore placed in Deuteromycetes. Later, its sexual stage was discovered. In this stage, two somatic cells of different strains fuse, karyogamy and meiosis occur in a single cell, and that cell then bears four spores on its outside. The fungus should now be placed in:","image":"","options":["Ascomycetes","Phycomycetes","Basidiomycetes","Deuteromycetes itself, since conidia are still produced"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Somatic plasmogamy followed by four exogenous spores from the cell where karyogamy and meiosis occurred describes a basidium bearing basidiospores, so the fungus belongs in Basidiomycetes. Once a perfect (sexual) stage is found, a fungus is moved out of Deuteromycetes into its rightful class.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following organisms completely lack a cell wall, are the smallest living cells known, and can survive without oxygen?","image":"","options":["Cyanobacteria","Mycoplasma","Methanogens","Diatoms"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes Mycoplasma as wall-less organisms that are the smallest living cells known and can survive without oxygen. Methanogens and cyanobacteria have cell walls, and diatoms have silica-embedded walls.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The spores of slime moulds possess true walls and can survive for many years even under adverse conditions.\nStatement II: The spores of slime moulds are dispersed mainly by water currents.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Slime mould spores do have true walls and are extremely resistant, so Statement I is correct. However, they are dispersed by air currents, not water, so Statement II is incorrect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"In cyanobacteria such as Nostoc and Anabaena, what is the function of the specialised cells called heterocysts?","image":"","options":["Storage of excess food as glycogen","Fixation of atmospheric nitrogen","Formation of spores during unfavourable conditions","Transfer of DNA from one bacterium to another"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that some cyanobacteria, for example Nostoc and Anabaena, fix atmospheric nitrogen in specialised cells called heterocysts. Spore formation and DNA transfer are general bacterial processes and are not linked to heterocysts.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Red tides, in which the sea appears red, are caused by the rapid multiplication of which organism?","image":"","options":["Euglena","Nostoc","Gonyaulax","Paramoecium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Gonyaulax is a red dinoflagellate. It can multiply so rapidly that the sea turns red, producing a red tide. Nostoc is a cyanobacterium that forms blooms in polluted water, not red tides.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Kingdom) with List-II (Nature of cell wall as per the five-kingdom comparison).\nList-I:\nA. Monera\nB. Protista\nC. Fungi\nD. Animalia\nList-II:\nI. Present, with chitin\nII. Absent\nIII. Noncellulosic (polysaccharide + amino acid)\nIV. Present in some\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The correct matches are:\n- Monera: noncellulosic wall made of polysaccharide and amino acid.\n- Protista: wall present only in some members.\n- Fungi: wall made of chitin.\n- Animalia: no cell wall.\nThe common mistake is swapping Monera and Protista.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"According to the comparative table of the five kingdoms, in which kingdom is the cell wall noncellulosic and made of polysaccharide + amino acid?","image":"","options":["Monera","Protista","Fungi","Plantae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Monera (prokaryotes) have a noncellulosic wall made of polysaccharide and amino acid. Fungi have chitin, and Plantae have cellulose.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In ascomycetes, conidia are produced exogenously on special hyphae called conidiophores.\nReason (R): In ascomycetes, ascospores are produced endogenously inside sac-like asci.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Both statements are correct according to NCERT. However, R describes the sexual spores (ascospores), while A describes the asexual spores (conidia), so R does not explain A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"In Whittaker's comparison of the five kingdoms, which kingdom has a non-cellulosic cell wall made of polysaccharide and amino acid?","image":"","options":["Fungi","Monera","Protista","Plantae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In Monera the cell wall is non-cellulosic, made of polysaccharide plus amino acid. Fungi have chitin, Plantae have cellulose, and among Protista only some members have a cell wall.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The boundaries of Kingdom Protista are well defined, and all biologists agree on which photosynthetic forms belong to it.\nStatement II: Chrysophytes include diatoms and golden algae (desmids), found in both fresh water and marine environments.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the boundaries of Protista are not well defined: one biologist's 'photosynthetic protistan' may be another's 'plant'. Statement II correctly describes chrysophytes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The body of Euglena is flexible.\nReason (R): Euglenoids lack a cell wall and are instead covered by a protein-rich pellicle.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Euglena has no rigid wall. Its covering is a protein-rich pellicle, and this is exactly what makes the body flexible. So R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (scientist) with List-II (contribution):\nList-I\n(a) Dmitri Ivanowsky (1892)\n(b) M.W. Beijerinck (1898)\n(c) W.M. Stanley (1935)\n(d) T.O. Diener (1971)\nList-II\n(i) Showed that viruses can be crystallised and the crystals are largely protein\n(ii) Discovered a free, low molecular weight RNA infectious agent\n(iii) Found that the agent of tobacco mosaic disease passes through bacteria-proof filters\n(iv) Named the new pathogen 'virus'","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The correct matches are:\n- Ivanowsky found that the tobacco mosaic agent passes through bacteria-proof filters.\n- Beijerinck named it 'virus'.\n- Stanley crystallised viruses and found the crystals were mostly protein.\n- Diener discovered the viroid.\n\nThe common trap is swapping Ivanowsky and Beijerinck.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"All eukaryotic unicellular organisms belong to","image":"","options":["Monera","Protista","Fungi","Bacteria"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Kingdom Protista includes all single-celled eukaryotes. Monera, which includes bacteria, consists of prokaryotes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 2 (MCQ Q1)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"White spots seen on mustard leaves are caused by a parasitic fungus. To which class does this fungus belong?","image":"","options":["Ascomycetes","Basidiomycetes","Deuteromycetes","Phycomycetes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The mustard parasite is Albugo, which belongs to the phycomycetes. Do not confuse it with rusts and smuts, which are basidiomycetes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following is NOT a member of Deuteromycetes?","image":"","options":["Alternaria","Colletotrichum","Agaricus","Trichoderma"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Agaricus, the mushroom, is a basidiomycete. Alternaria, Colletotrichum and Trichoderma are the examples of deuteromycetes given in NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"A marine biologist finds a coastal sea turned red, with many dead fish. Microscopic examination shows huge numbers of a single-celled photosynthetic organism. Its outer wall is made of stiff cellulose plates, and it has two flagella: one longitudinal and one lying in a transverse groove between the plates. Which is the most likely identification and cause of the fish deaths?","image":"","options":["Diatoms; their silica shells clog the gills of fishes","Dinoflagellates such as Gonyaulax; toxins released by their large numbers","Euglenoids; their pellicle makes them predate on fishes","Cyanobacteria; heterocysts release excess nitrogen that poisons fishes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Cellulose wall plates and a flagellum in a transverse furrow identify a dinoflagellate. Red dinoflagellates such as Gonyaulax cause red tides, and the toxins released by such large populations can kill fishes. Diatoms have silica shells and do not cause red tides.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Slime moulds can survive unfavourable conditions for many years.\nReason (R): During unfavourable conditions, the plasmodium differentiates into fruiting bodies that bear spores with true walls, which are extremely resistant.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Under adverse conditions the plasmodium forms fruiting bodies bearing resistant, true-walled spores. These spores can survive for many years and are dispersed by air currents, so R directly explains A. The trap is to think the plasmodium itself survives; it actually forms under suitable conditions.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The mycelium of Mucor is aseptate and coenocytic.\n(ii) Aplanospores of phycomycetes are motile asexual spores.\n(iii) In ascomycetes, ascospores are produced exogenously.\n(iv) Yeast (Saccharomyces) is a unicellular member of the sac fungi.\n(v) In phycomycetes, a zygospore may result from the fusion of morphologically dissimilar gametes.\n(vi) Aspergillus and Claviceps have branched, septate mycelium.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (iv), (v) and (vi) are correct, because phycomycete gametes may be isogamous, anisogamous or oogamous. Statement (ii) is wrong because aplanospores are non-motile; zoospores are the motile ones. Statement (iii) is wrong because ascospores form endogenously inside asci.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"A student examines the gill of a mushroom under a microscope. She sees a cell bearing four spores attached on its outer surface. These spores are:","image":"","options":["Ascospores","Conidia","Zoospores","Basidiospores"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"In mushrooms, each basidium produces four basidiospores exogenously, on its outer surface. Ascospores are formed endogenously inside asci, and zoospores are motile spores of phycomycetes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the genetic material of viruses is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Viruses that infect plants generally have single-stranded RNA","Bacteriophages are usually double-stranded DNA viruses","Animal viruses may have single- or double-stranded RNA, or double-stranded DNA","Some viruses carry both RNA and DNA, which together make the virus infectious"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"No virus contains both RNA and DNA. A virus has one type of nucleic acid, and it is this genetic material that is infectious. The other three statements match the general pattern of viral genetic material by host type.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT described in NCERT as a symptom of viral infection in plants?","image":"","options":["Mosaic formation and yellowing","Leaf rolling and curling","Vein clearing and dwarfing","White spots on leaves caused by a parasite that produces zoospores"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT lists these viral symptoms in plants: mosaic formation, leaf rolling and curling, yellowing, vein clearing, dwarfing and stunted growth. White spots on mustard leaves are caused by the fungus Albugo, a phycomycete, which reproduces by zoospores.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following fungi belongs to the class Deuteromycetes?","image":"","options":["Ustilago","Trichoderma","Agaricus","Puccinia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Trichoderma is a deuteromycete (imperfect fungus), along with Alternaria and Colletotrichum. Ustilago (smut), Agaricus (mushroom) and Puccinia (rust) are all basidiomycetes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Viruses are non-cellular organisms but replicate themselves once they infect the host cell. To which of the following kingdom do viruses belong to?","image":"","options":["Monera","Protista","Fungi","None of the above"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Viruses are acellular and are not truly living outside a host cell. For this reason they are not placed in any of Whittaker's five kingdoms.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 2 (MCQ Q11)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Holozoic nutrition\n(b) Cell wall mainly of cellulose\n(c) Cell wall of chitin\n(d) Noncellulosic wall of polysaccharide and amino acid\nList-II\n(i) Monera\n(ii) Fungi\n(iii) Animalia\n(iv) Plantae","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i","a-iii, b-ii, c-iv, d-i","a-ii, b-iv, c-iii, d-i","a-i, b-iv, c-ii, d-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Animals ingest their food (holozoic nutrition), and plants have cellulose walls. Fungi have chitin walls, while Monera have noncellulosic walls made of polysaccharide and amino acid.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Most euglenoids are freshwater organisms found in stagnant water.\n(ii) The pigments of euglenoids differ from those present in higher plants.\n(iii) The plasmodium of a slime mould may grow and spread over several feet.\n(iv) Slime moulds form fruiting bodies during favourable conditions.\n(v) Slime mould spores are extremely resistant and can survive for many years.\n(vi) Euglena has a cellulose cell wall.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (iii) and (v) are correct, so the count is 3. Euglenoid pigments are identical to those of higher plants, so (ii) is wrong. Fruiting bodies form under unfavourable conditions, so (iv) is wrong. Euglena has a pellicle rather than a cell wall, so (vi) is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about chrysophytes and dinoflagellates:\nStatement I: Dinoflagellates usually have two flagella, one lying longitudinally and the other transversely in a furrow between the cellulose wall plates.\nStatement II: Red tides are caused by the rapid multiplication of diatoms, and the gritty deposits these diatoms leave behind are used for filtering oils and syrups.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I correctly describes the arrangement of the two flagella in dinoflagellates. Statement II is wrong because red tides are caused by red dinoflagellates such as Gonyaulax, not by diatoms, even though diatomaceous earth is indeed used in filtration.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In ascomycetes and basidiomycetes, cells formed after the fusion of two compatible haploid hyphae contain two nuclei each.\nReason (R): In these fungi, karyogamy does not immediately follow plasmogamy.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In these fungi plasmogamy is not followed at once by karyogamy, so the two parental nuclei remain separate. This gives an n + n (dikaryon) condition, so R correctly explains A. The parental nuclei fuse later to form diploid cells.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"A fungus has the following features:\n1. Branched, septate mycelium\n2. Asexual spores borne exogenously on specialised hyphae\n3. Sexual spores formed endogenously inside sac-like cells\n4. These sac-like cells are grouped within fruiting bodies\nThe fungus is most likely:","image":"","options":["Rhizopus","Aspergillus","Agaricus","Albugo"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"These features describe an ascomycete: conidia on conidiophores, and ascospores in asci arranged in ascocarps. Aspergillus is an ascomycete. Rhizopus and Albugo have aseptate mycelium, and Agaricus bears its sexual spores (basidiospores) exogenously.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"In which group of protozoans do the marine forms have silica shells on their surface?","image":"","options":["Ciliated protozoans","Flagellated protozoans","Amoeboid protozoans","Sporozoans"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that marine amoeboid protozoans bear silica shells on their surface. Do not confuse this with diatoms, which are chrysophytes, not protozoans, although their walls are also embedded with silica.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Archaebacteria can live in habitats such as extremely salty areas and hot springs, where very few other organisms survive.\nReason (R): Archaebacteria have a cell wall structure that differs from that of other bacteria.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the distinct cell wall structure of archaebacteria is responsible for their survival in extreme conditions. So R directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"In the filamentous cyanobacteria Nostoc and Anabaena, what is the main role of the specialised cells called heterocysts?","image":"","options":["Storage of reserve food as glycogen","Fixation of atmospheric nitrogen","Formation of spores under unfavourable conditions","Secretion of the gelatinous sheath around the colony"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Heterocysts are the specialised cells in which Nostoc and Anabaena fix atmospheric nitrogen. The gelatinous sheath surrounds colonies but is not made by heterocysts.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Kingdom Protista places Chlamydomonas and Chlorella in the same kingdom as Amoeba and Paramoecium, although these organisms belonged to different kingdoms in earlier classifications.\nReason (R): In Whittaker's system, organisms are grouped into Protista mainly because they possess a cell wall.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: unicellular eukaryotes with walls (Chlamydomonas, Chlorella) and without walls (Amoeba, Paramoecium) are now grouped together in Protista. R is false: grouping by the presence of a cell wall was the basis of the older 'Plants' grouping. Protista instead unites single-celled eukaryotes, regardless of whether they have a wall.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following diseases are caused by bacteria?\nCholera, Mumps, Typhoid, Malaria, Tetanus, Influenza, Citrus canker, Sleeping sickness","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Cholera, typhoid, tetanus and citrus canker are bacterial diseases. Mumps and influenza are caused by viruses. Malaria (Plasmodium) and sleeping sickness (Trypanosoma) are caused by protozoans.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about Basidiomycetes are correct?\n(i) Asexual spores are generally not found.\n(ii) Sex organs are absent; plasmogamy occurs by fusion of two somatic cells of different strains.\n(iii) Basidiospores are produced endogenously inside the basidium.\n(iv) Karyogamy and meiosis in the basidium produce four basidiospores.\n(v) The mycelium is aseptate and coenocytic.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii) and (iv) are correct. Basidiospores are produced exogenously on the basidium, so (iii) is wrong. An aseptate, coenocytic mycelium is a feature of phycomycetes, not basidiomycetes, so (v) is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which sequence correctly describes the sexual cycle of Agaricus?","image":"","options":["Fusion of gametes from sex organs → karyogamy → dikaryon → meiosis → four ascospores","Fusion of two somatic cells of different strains → dikaryotic stage → karyogamy in basidium → meiosis → four basidiospores","Fusion of two somatic cells → karyogamy immediately → meiosis in zygospore → many basidiospores","Plasmogamy of motile gametes → dikaryotic stage → meiosis → karyogamy → four basidiospores"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Basidiomycetes lack sex organs, so plasmogamy occurs by fusion of somatic cells of different strains, giving a dikaryotic stage. Karyogamy and then meiosis follow in the basidium, producing four basidiospores; karyogamy must come before meiosis.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Basidiomycetes reproduce sexually even though they lack sex organs.\nReason (R): In basidiomycetes, plasmogamy is brought about by the fusion of two vegetative or somatic cells of different strains or genotypes.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Basidiomycetes have no sex organs, so somatic cells of different strains fuse to achieve plasmogamy. This fusion is exactly what allows them to reproduce sexually, so R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"In Euglena, the body remains flexible because instead of a cell wall it possesses a:","image":"","options":["Silica-embedded shell made of two overlapping halves","Protein-rich layer called pellicle","Wall of stiff cellulose plates","Chitinous wall with polysaccharides"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Euglenoids lack a cell wall and have a protein-rich pellicle, which keeps the body flexible. Silica shells belong to diatoms, cellulose plates to dinoflagellates, and chitin walls to fungi.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"With respect to fungal sexual cycle, choose the correct sequence of events","image":"","options":["Karyogamy, Plasmogamy and Meiosis","Meiosis, Plasmogamy and Karyogamy","Plasmogamy, Karyogamy and Meiosis","Meiosis, Karyogamy and Plasmogamy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The fungal sexual cycle begins with plasmogamy, the fusion of protoplasm. Karyogamy, the fusion of nuclei, follows, and the zygote then undergoes meiosis to produce haploid spores.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 2 (MCQ Q10)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Sleeping sickness is caused by a parasitic protozoan that belongs to which group?","image":"","options":["Sporozoans, e.g., Plasmodium","Ciliated protozoans, e.g., Paramoecium","Flagellated protozoans, e.g., Trypanosoma","Amoeboid protozoans, e.g., Entamoeba"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Trypanosoma is a parasitic flagellated protozoan that causes sleeping sickness. Plasmodium is the sporozoan that causes malaria.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Mycoplasma can survive in the absence of oxygen.\nReason (R): Mycoplasma completely lack a cell wall.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Both statements are facts given in NCERT. However, the ability to live without oxygen is a metabolic feature and is not explained by the absence of a cell wall.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Bacteria reproduce mainly by fission and may form spores under unfavourable conditions.\nStatement II: The sexual reproduction of bacteria involves fusion of male and female gametes to form a diploid zygote.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Fission is the main method of bacterial reproduction, and spores are formed under unfavourable conditions. The bacterial 'sort of sexual reproduction' is only a primitive transfer of DNA from one bacterium to another, not fusion of gametes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which group of organisms is described in NCERT as the chief 'producers' in the oceans?","image":"","options":["Dinoflagellates","Diatoms","Slime moulds","Euglenoids"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Diatoms are photosynthetic chrysophytes and are the chief producers in the oceans. Dinoflagellates are also mostly marine and photosynthetic, but NCERT gives the 'chief producer' title to diatoms.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"In members of ascomycetes such as Penicillium, how are the asexual spores produced?","image":"","options":["Sporangiospores produced endogenously in a sporangium","Ascospores produced endogenously in asci","Conidia produced exogenously on conidiophores","Basidiospores produced exogenously on a basidium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In sac fungi, the asexual spores are conidia, which form exogenously on special hyphae called conidiophores. Ascospores are also produced by ascomycetes, but they are sexual spores and form endogenously inside asci.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"The white spots seen on mustard leaves are caused by a parasitic fungus that belongs to phycomycetes. Which fungus is this?","image":"","options":["Puccinia","Albugo","Ustilago","Claviceps"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Albugo is the phycomycete that parasitises mustard and causes white spots on its leaves. Puccinia (rust) and Ustilago (smut) are basidiomycetes, and Claviceps is an ascomycete.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about cyanobacteria are correct?\n(i) They may be unicellular, colonial or filamentous.\n(ii) Their colonies are generally surrounded by a gelatinous sheath.\n(iii) They often form blooms in polluted water bodies.\n(iv) All cyanobacteria fix atmospheric nitrogen.\n(v) They are eukaryotic algae placed under Protista.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii) and (iii) are correct. Only some cyanobacteria (such as Nostoc and Anabaena) fix nitrogen, so (iv) is wrong. Cyanobacteria are prokaryotic eubacteria under Monera, so (v) is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about Kingdom Monera is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The cell wall of monerans is noncellulosic, made of polysaccharide and amino acid","Bacteria are structurally simple but very complex in behaviour","Archaebacteria differ from eubacteria mainly in completely lacking a cell wall","Methanogens occur both in marshy areas and in the gut of ruminant animals"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Archaebacteria do have a cell wall; what sets them apart is its different structure. Complete absence of a cell wall is a feature of Mycoplasma. The other three statements agree with NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which scientist showed that the extract of infected tobacco plants could infect healthy plants, and called this infectious fluid 'Contagium vivum fluidum'?","image":"","options":["Dmitri Ivanowsky","M.W. Beijerinck","W.M. Stanley","T.O. Diener"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In 1898 Beijerinck showed that sap from infected tobacco plants could transmit the disease to healthy plants. He named the pathogen 'virus' and the fluid Contagium vivum fluidum (infectious living fluid). Ivanowsky came earlier and only found that the agent passed through bacteria-proof filters.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"With respect to mode of nutrition, the vast majority of bacteria are:","image":"","options":["Photosynthetic autotrophs","Chemosynthetic autotrophs","Heterotrophs","Holozoic organisms"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Some bacteria are autotrophs, either photosynthetic or chemosynthetic, but the vast majority are heterotrophs. They depend on other organisms or on dead organic matter for food. Holozoic nutrition (ingestion) is characteristic of animals.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Difference between Virus and Viroid is","image":"","options":["Absence of protein coat in viroid but present in virus","Presence of low molecular weight RNA in virus but absent in viroid","Both a and b","None of the above"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A viroid is free RNA that lacks the protein coat found in viruses. It is the viroid, not the virus, that has low molecular weight RNA, so statement b is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 2 (MCQ Q9)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"In members of Kingdom Animalia, food reserves are stored mainly as:","image":"","options":["Starch","Glycogen or fat","Cellulose","Chitin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Animals store food reserves as glycogen or fat. Starch is the typical plant reserve, while cellulose and chitin are cell wall materials, not reserves.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are groups of archaebacteria?\nHalophiles, Cyanobacteria, Thermoacidophiles, Mycoplasma, Methanogens, Nostoc","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Only halophiles, thermoacidophiles and methanogens are archaebacteria. Cyanobacteria, including Nostoc, are eubacteria. Mycoplasma are wall-less organisms, not archaebacteria.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"The five kingdom classification was proposed by","image":"","options":["R.H. Whittaker","C.Linnaeus","A. Roxberg","Virchow"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R.H. Whittaker proposed the five kingdom system in 1969. Its kingdoms are Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae and Animalia.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 2 (MCQ Q2)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"With reference to Whittaker's five kingdom comparison, which of the following statements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Monera have a noncellulosic cell wall made of polysaccharide and amino acid","Fungi have a cell wall containing chitin and show multicellular or loose tissue body organisation","All members of Protista possess a cell wall, as they were earlier placed among algae","Animalia lack a cell wall and show a tissue/organ/organ-system level of organisation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A cell wall is present in only some protists. Protista includes wall-less forms such as Amoeba and Paramoecium, which were earlier placed in the animal kingdom. The other options match the comparative table.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"A student examines a sample from a polluted pond showing a bloom. Under the microscope the organism is prokaryotic, has a rigid cell wall, is photosynthetic, and forms filaments enclosed in a gelatinous sheath. A few distinctive specialised cells are seen at intervals along each filament. Which inference is correct?","image":"","options":["It is a golden alga of Kingdom Protista with silica walls","It lacks a cell wall and is related to Mycoplasma","It is a cyanobacterium like Nostoc, and the specialised cells can fix atmospheric nitrogen","It is an archaebacterium whose wall structure allows survival in hot springs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Several features point to a cyanobacterium such as Nostoc: a prokaryote with a rigid wall, photosynthesis, filaments in a gelatinous sheath, and blooms in polluted water. The specialised cells are heterocysts, which fix nitrogen. Golden algae are eukaryotic, and Mycoplasma have no cell wall.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-178","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":178,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Aplanospores of phycomycetes are non-motile and are produced endogenously in a sporangium","Morels and truffles are edible ascomycetes considered delicacies","Ascocarps are fruiting bodies in which the conidia of sac fungi are arranged","Members of phycomycetes may occur as obligate parasites on plants"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Ascocarps contain the asci, which bear the sexual ascospores. Conidia are asexual spores borne on conidiophores, not inside ascocarps. All the other statements agree with NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-179","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":179,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the three-domain system mentioned in NCERT?","image":"","options":["It divides Kingdom Protista into two domains and places all prokaryotes in the third domain","It divides Kingdom Monera into two domains, places the remaining eukaryotic kingdoms in the third domain, and thus gives a six kingdom classification","It merges Fungi and Plantae into one domain, giving a four kingdom classification","It divides Kingdom Animalia into vertebrates and invertebrates, giving a six kingdom classification"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The three-domain system splits Monera (prokaryotes) into two domains and keeps all eukaryotic kingdoms in the third domain. The prokaryotes thus become two kingdoms, giving six kingdoms in total.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-180","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":180,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Diatomaceous earth\nB. Red tide\nC. Plankton\nD. Transverse furrow\nList-II\n(i) Organisms that float passively in water currents\n(ii) Gritty deposit used in polishing and filtering oils and syrups\n(iii) Groove between wall plates that holds one flagellum of a dinoflagellate\n(iv) Rapid multiplication of Gonyaulax\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Diatomaceous earth is a gritty deposit used for polishing and filtration. Red tides result from Gonyaulax blooms. Plankton float passively in water currents. In dinoflagellates, one flagellum lies transversely in a furrow between the wall plates.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-181","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":181,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Anabaena\n(B) Mycoplasma\n(C) Chemosynthetic autotrophic bacteria\n(D) Heterotrophic bacteria\nList-II:\n(I) Smallest living cells known\n(II) Oxidise ammonia, nitrites and nitrates for ATP production\n(III) Conversion of milk into curd\n(IV) Nitrogen fixation in heterocysts\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Anabaena fixes nitrogen in heterocysts, and Mycoplasma are the smallest living cells. Chemosynthetic bacteria gain energy by oxidising inorganic nitrogen compounds, and heterotrophic bacteria help in making curd from milk.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-182","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":182,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Which ascomycete is used extensively in biochemical and genetic work?","image":"","options":["Claviceps","Aspergillus","Saccharomyces","Neurospora"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT names Neurospora as the sac fungus used extensively in biochemical and genetic research. The other options are also ascomycetes, but none of them is the one given this role in the text.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-183","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":183,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Halophiles\nB. Thermoacidophiles\nC. Methanogens\nD. Chemosynthetic autotrophic bacteria\nList-II\nI. Hot springs\nII. Oxidise ammonia, nitrites and nitrates for ATP production\nIII. Extremely salty areas\nIV. Marshy areas and the gut of ruminants\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Halophiles live in salty areas and thermoacidophiles in hot springs. Methanogens occur in marshes and the ruminant gut. Chemosynthetic autotrophs oxidise inorganic substances such as ammonia and nitrites to make ATP.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-184","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":184,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following diseases are caused by viruses?\nMumps, Typhoid, Small pox, Citrus canker, Herpes, Influenza, Tetanus, AIDS, Mad cow disease","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mumps, small pox, herpes, influenza and AIDS are viral, which makes five. Typhoid, tetanus and citrus canker are bacterial. Mad cow disease (BSE) is caused by a prion, not a virus.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-185","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":185,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Zygospore\n(B) Ascocarp\n(C) Conidiophore\n(D) Sporangium\nList-II\n(I) Fruiting body in which asci are arranged\n(II) Special mycelium bearing exogenously formed asexual spores\n(III) Structure formed by fusion of two gametes in phycomycetes\n(IV) Structure in which zoospores or aplanospores form endogenously\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A zygospore forms when two gametes fuse in phycomycetes, and asci are arranged in fruiting bodies called ascocarps. Conidiophores bear conidia exogenously, while zoospores and aplanospores develop endogenously inside a sporangium.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BIO-CLASSIFICATION-186","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biological Classification","qno":186,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biological Classification","type":"mcq","question":"Cyanobacteria are photosynthetic autotrophs because they possess:","image":"","options":["Chlorophyll a, similar to that of green plants","Bacteriochlorophyll only, unlike green plants","Chloroplasts bounded by a double membrane","Pigments identical to those of red dinoflagellates"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT describes cyanobacteria as having chlorophyll a similar to green plants. Being prokaryotes, they do not have membrane-bound chloroplasts.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 2 · Biological Classification","difficulty":"easy"}]});
